# Postgres connection (docker-compose default below).
#
# IMPORTANT for multi-business deployments (Phase 12): tenant isolation is
# enforced by Postgres row-level security, which superusers and BYPASSRLS
# roles ignore entirely — every policy becomes a silent no-op. The `pos` role
# that docker-compose creates IS a superuser, so it is fine for local
# single-business development but must NOT be what a hosted deployment
# connects as. Provision an unprivileged role and point this at it:
#
#   APP_DB_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -hex 32) npm run db:app-role
#   DATABASE_URL=postgres://pos_app:<that password>@localhost:5432/pos
#
# Migrations keep running as the owner (they create tables; the app must not).
# The server refuses to start in production if this role can bypass RLS.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://pos:pos@localhost:5432/pos

# Password for the unprivileged application role created by `npm run db:app-role`.
# Only needed when running that script.
# APP_DB_PASSWORD=

# Role name for the same script. Defaults to pos_app.
# APP_DB_USER=pos_app

# Secret used to sign JWT session cookies. MUST be changed in production.
# Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
JWT_SECRET=change-me-in-production

# Owner/Manager session lifetime (hours). Default 12.
SESSION_HOURS=12

# Biometric login / WebAuthn (Phase 20 Wave 3). One deployment is one domain
# (see JWT_SECRET above — the whole app already shares one signing key across
# every business), so one RP ID/origin pair covers every business too.
# WEBAUTHN_RP_ID: the domain staff sign in on (no scheme/port) — defaults to
#   "localhost" for local development.
# WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME: shown by the OS/browser's own biometric prompt.
# WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN: the exact origin(s) (scheme+host+port) requests will come
#   from, comma-separated if there is more than one (e.g. a staging + prod
#   domain) — defaults to "http://localhost:3000". Must match what the
#   browser's address bar shows exactly, or every ceremony fails verification.
# WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=localhost
# WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME=سیستم فروش کافه و رستوران
# WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# Self-service business registration (Phase 12). Off unless set to exactly
# "true": a deployment being *capable* of many businesses doesn't mean anyone
# may create one. Leave unset for on-premise installs; the super-admin console
# (Phase 15) provisions businesses regardless of this setting.
# ALLOW_PUBLIC_SIGNUP=false

# Bidirectional server-to-server sync (Phase 11) — lets a café laptop (local
# server) and the VPS keep each other in sync so the café keeps working during
# an internet outage and catches up on reconnect.
#
# REMOTE_SYNC_TOKEN is the shared bearer token that guards THIS server's
#   /api/server-sync/push and /pull endpoints (i.e. set it on the server that
#   *receives* sync — typically the VPS, but set it on both if both accept).
#   Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# The peer's URL + the same token are configured per-business in the Owner
#   dashboard (settings key server_sync.config), not here. Since Phase 23 the
#   dashboard generates that token itself (POS1-… format) — there is no longer
#   any reason to run openssl by hand for it.
# REMOTE_SYNC_TOKEN=

# Which side of the sync link this install is (Phase 23).
#
#   central — the VPS everyone syncs TO. Shows no "connect me to a central
#             server" form, and refuses writes to server_sync.config.
#   site    — a café laptop or desktop install that syncs to a central server.
#             Its central-server address is derived from pairing, not typed.
#
# When unset the role is inferred exactly as the code inferred it before this
# variable existed — central if REMOTE_SYNC_TOKEN or POS_DOMAIN is set, else
# site — so no existing deployment changes behaviour. The server logs which it
# chose, and where from, at startup.
# DEPLOYMENT_ROLE=site

# Base URL of the central server/platform, e.g. https://pos.eshobe.com. Used
# to derive the sync target on a site that has not been paired yet. Defaults
# to https://$POS_DOMAIN when that is set.
# PLATFORM_BASE_URL=

# Per-business subdomains (Phase 23).
#
# ROOT_DOMAIN is the zone every business origin hangs off. Each business is
# served from {subdomain}.$ROOT_DOMAIN, the super-admin console from
# admin.$ROOT_DOMAIN, and the bare domain becomes a "which business?" router.
# Locally use `localtest.me`: every subdomain of it resolves to 127.0.0.1 over
# plain HTTP, so no hosts-file editing is needed.
#
# SUBDOMAIN_ROUTING is the switch. Off (the default) keeps the pre-Phase-21
# behaviour, where a business is identified by a /{slug}/dashboard path prefix
# on one shared origin. Turn it on only once DNS and the *wildcard* certificate
# are confirmed — Let's Encrypt will not issue a wildcard over HTTP-01, so the
# Traefik certresolver must use a DNS-01 challenge.
#
# With it on, a session cookie is valid only on its own business's host: that
# is the isolation this setting buys, and it is why the cookie deliberately
# carries no `domain` attribute (see sessionCookieOptions in auth-edge.ts).
# ROOT_DOMAIN=localtest.me
# SUBDOMAIN_ROUTING=off

# AI assistant (Phase 18) — one platform-owned provider connection for every
# business. Configure it normally in /platform/ai; these values are only an
# operator-level bootstrap fallback when the singleton DB row has not been
# saved yet. They are never read from a business dashboard.
#
# Both providers speak the OpenAI-compatible /chat/completions shape. Billing
# requires positive rate/reservation/unit values before the assistant can run.
# AI_ENABLED=false
# AI_PROVIDER=openrouter
# AI_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o-mini
# AI_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# AI_TEMPERATURE=0.3
# AI_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=1000
# AI_INPUT_TOKEN_RIAL_PER_MILLION=
# AI_OUTPUT_TOKEN_RIAL_PER_MILLION=
# AI_MAX_TURN_RIAL=
# AI_CREDIT_UNIT_RIAL=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# ARVAN_AI_API_KEY=
# APP_URL=https://your-pos.example.com

# Used by scripts/seed.ts for the initial Owner account
SEED_OWNER_EMAIL=owner@example.com
SEED_OWNER_PASSWORD=owner1234
SEED_OWNER_NAME=مالک نمونه

# Print agent (Phase 5) — a separate process (`npm run print-agent`) meant to
# run on the till PC itself, next to the printer/cash drawer. The dashboard's
# browser talks to it directly on localhost, not through this app's server.
# PRINT_AGENT_PORT: the agent's own loopback HTTP port (default 9123).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PRINT_AGENT_URL: what the browser calls (must match, if set).
# PRINT_AGENT_CHROMIUM_PATH: Chromium executable the agent uses to render
#   receipts/tickets (correct Persian/RTL shaping needs a real browser
#   engine — see src/lib/escpos.ts). Point it at wherever
#   `npx playwright install chromium` (or a system Chromium/Chrome) landed.
# PRINT_AGENT_PORT=9123
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PRINT_AGENT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9123
# PRINT_AGENT_CHROMIUM_PATH=/path/to/chromium

# Backups (Phase 10) — schedule/retention/cloud settings live in the Owner
# dashboard (پشتیبان‌گیری); these env vars are machine-level paths/overrides.
# BACKUP_DIR: where pg_dump artifacts are written (default: ./backups).
# BACKUP_SECONDARY_DIR: optional second local target (mounted USB drive/NAS);
#   when set, every backup is also copied there and a failed copy fails the
#   run (so an unplugged drive surfaces on the dashboard).
# PG_DUMP_PATH / PG_RESTORE_PATH: binaries to use if not on PATH (the host
#   needs postgresql-client version >= the server's major version).
# BACKUP_DATABASE_URL: connection pg_dump uses. The app itself connects as the
#   restricted pos_app role (row-level security applies to it), and pg_dump
#   fails on such a role — "query would be affected by row-level security
#   policy for table ..." — so backups need the privileged/owner connection,
#   the same one migrations run with. Docker's entrypoint and the desktop
#   launcher set this automatically; set it by hand only if DATABASE_URL isn't
#   a privileged connection (e.g. managed Postgres where you provisioned
#   pos_app yourself).
# BACKUP_DIR=./backups
# BACKUP_SECONDARY_DIR=/mnt/usb-backup
# PG_DUMP_PATH=pg_dump
# PG_RESTORE_PATH=pg_restore
# BACKUP_DATABASE_URL=postgres://pos:pos@localhost:5432/pos

# Restoring from cloud after total machine loss (scripts/restore.ts
# --from-cloud) — there is no local DB to read the dashboard's cloud settings
# from, so the S3 credentials and the encryption passphrase come from env:
# BACKUP_PASSPHRASE=
# BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=
# BACKUP_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=
# BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
